WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.600
Eddie Brock and Venom are back for the sequel
to the 2018 hit about the symbiotic antihero,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.030
and like Cletus Kasady promised, there's gonna
be carnage.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:14.330
Here's everything you might have missed in
the ultra-violent first trailer for "Venom:

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.330
Let There Be Carnage."

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.640
We've known for a while that Woody Harrelson
would bring Cletus Kasady to life in "Venom:

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.279
Let There Be Carnage," following up the cameo
appearance he made at the end of the first

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:25.279
film.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:28.539
The trailer indicates that he's going to be
quite the formidable opponent for the film's

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:32.880
titular anti-hero, as it teases a big showdown
between the pair, and it's safe to assume

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.730
that much scenery will be chewed in the process.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.620
In the comics, Eddie Brock and Cletus Kasady
have a history with one another.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:46.260
First introduced in 1991's "The Amazing Spider-Man"
344, Kasady is a sociopathic serial killer

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.510
who becomes the host for Carnage, an alien
symbiote who's much nastier than Venom.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.740
Due to Kasady's lack of moral compass as a
human, things get even worse when Carnage

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:56.860
is part of the equation.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:01.860
Together, the pair have been guilty of some
pretty heinous crimes throughout the years.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.140
Kasady is depicted as somewhat of a sympathetic
character in the Marvel comics, as he had

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.830
a tough upbringing courtesy of his abusive
mother and grandmother.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:14.240
It will be interesting to see how "Let There
Be Carnage" explores his troubled backstory

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.490
and his connection to Eddie.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.170
The trailer for "Let There Be Carnage" offers
a brief look at the entrance to the Ravencroft

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.840
Institute for the Criminally Insane, where
Cletus Kasady is being held.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.350
The notorious supervillain was actually born
in the asylum to his mother Louise Kasady,

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:33.810
as explained in 2018's "Web of Venom: Carnage
Born" 1.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.510
Kasady finds himself back at his birthplace
after he's captured for heinous crimes.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:42.200
There, he meets his love interest Shriek,
aka Frances Barrison, who also makes a brief

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.170
appearance in the trailer — but more on
her later.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.350
In the Marvel comics, Kasady's actions lead
to the destruction of Ravencroft, which appears

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.060
to happen in the trailer as well.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.370
It looks like Kasady is going to be executed
by lethal injection, but something goes wrong,

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.890
as Kasady is screaming in pain and the windows
of the room he's being held in shatter.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.960
The trailer then cuts to show a massive building
up in flames, and we're led to believe this

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.229
is Ravencroft.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:08.399
As it happens, there's comic book precedence
for this incident.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:13.580
Famous Marvel villain Wilson Fisk funds the
cleanup of the asylum in 2020's "Ruins of

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:18.620
Ravencroft" 1, but there's some interference
from Reed Richards, aka Mr. Fantastic, of

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.080
the Fantastic Four.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.459
Richards wants to ensure that the new facility
is going to be used properly, and is a thorn

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:25.840
in Fisk's side.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.840
In "Ravencroft" 1, the Daredevil villain hires
Norman Osborn and other famous Marvel baddies

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.499
to work in the resurrected asylum.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.909
Marvel fans have long known how important
"The Daily Bugle" is in the sprawling world

00:02:37.909 --> 00:02:43.109
of Spider-Man, and since both Venom and Carnage
are established Spidey villains in the comics,

00:02:43.109 --> 00:02:47.629
it's fitting that "Venom: Let There Be Carnage"
reference the newspaper-turned-online-outlet.

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:51.620
In the trailer, Eddie visits the "Daily Bugle"
website, and in the next scene, Detective

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.640
Mulligan is reading a hard copy of the “West
Coast Edition” of the newspaper.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.890
In the comics, both Eddie and Peter Parker
work for newspapers.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.249
In fact, Eddie sees Peter as a rival, which
leads to him becoming one of Spider-Man's

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:06.620
most dangerous enemies when he inherits the
Venom symbiote.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.029
While "The Daily Bugle" played a key role
in Sam Raimi's Spider-Man movies, the only

00:03:11.029 --> 00:03:15.250
major "Daily Bugle" moment in the Tom Holland
movies occurred at the end of "Spider-Man:

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.400
Far From Home," when the paper's editor, J.
Jonah Jameson, released a video revealing

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.279
Peter's secret identity.

00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:25.019
These two references in the trailer make it
clear that the newspaper is part of the "Venom"

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:29.109
franchise just as it is in the "Spider-Man"
films, but that doesn't mean all the characters

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.620
are aware of one another's existence.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.809
Director Andy Serkis told IGN that Eddie is,
quote, “unaware at this point of other characters

00:03:35.809 --> 00:03:36.809
like Spider-Man.”

00:03:36.809 --> 00:03:38.019
Serkis explained,

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:41.639
"Obviously, there are links between Venom
and Spider-Man in the Marvel Universe and

00:03:41.639 --> 00:03:46.469
the Spider-Man story, but in this, we're treating
this very much as it's his own world, the

00:03:46.469 --> 00:03:48.419
Venom story is his own world.”

00:03:49.419 --> 00:03:53.069
In the trailer, we see Detective Mulligan
reading "The Daily Bugle," with a headline

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:55.730
that highlights Cletus Kasady's "hidden victims."

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.650
While Mulligan may seem like your typical
detective whose primary goal is to get justice

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.699
for Kasady's victims and lock up the villain
for life, there might be more to him than

00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:04.700
meets the eye.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:08.879
In the pages of Marvel Comics, there's a character
named Patrick Mulligan, who works as a police

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:13.170
officer and ends up becoming the host for
the terrifying symbiote Toxin, which has all

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.639
of Venom and Carnage's powers but heightened
to a greater degree.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:19.919
Could we see Toxin bind to Detective Mulligan
in "Venom 2"?

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:20.919
Never say never.

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:25.110
Watch carefully as Mulligan shuts the newspaper,
and you'll spot a headline on another page

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.930
that seemingly reads, "Avengers Lose to Nightmare."

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.449
This could be a blanket statement or an incomplete
headline — the Avengers have lost a battle

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:37.490
to a nightmarish villain, or they lost in
a nightmare of a situation — be a nod to

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.490
the Marvel Comics supervillain Nightmare,
who might play a part in "Doctor Strange in

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:42.490
the Multiverse of Madness."

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:48.389
Now, behind Mulligan in this specific shot
is a wanted poster for a man named E. Larson.

00:04:48.389 --> 00:04:52.590
Erik Larsen, spelled with an "E" instead of
an "O," is a popular name in Marvel Comics

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.650
and has strong ties to Spider-Man.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.919
The author, publisher, and artist's first
big works for Marvel came with 1990's "The

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:02.930
Amazing Spider-Man" 239, for which he designed
the cover art.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:07.430
A year later, in 1991, Larsen created the
character of Cletus Kasady in "The Amazing

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.949
Spider-Man" 344.

00:05:09.949 --> 00:05:14.310
The wanted poster in the trailer appears to
be a nod to the former Marvel writer, and

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.840
the intentional misspelling of his name might
either be Sony Pictures' way of throwing a

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.400
little bit of shade at Larsen or making the
reference a bit less obvious.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.710
Larsen left Marvel Comics in 1992 to create
the comic book publishing company Image Comics.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.900
Still alive and well today, Image Comics is
the publisher behind “Spawn,” "The Walking

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.849
Dead," "Invincible," and "Kick-Ass."

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:38.290
Other former Marvel names who left with Larsen
include Todd McFarlane and Rob Liefield, who

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.080
co-created Deadpool and Cable.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:44.979
In the trailer for "Venom: Let There Be Carnage,"
we see Cletus Kasady in what appears to be

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:47.189
his prison cell, writing on a postcard.

00:05:47.189 --> 00:05:50.860
This postcard appears to be similar to one
we see Eddie holding while he sits down to

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.811
eat breakfast at the beginning of the trailer.

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:57.129
The postcards we see are covered with drawings
and dense writing, which might provide an

00:05:57.129 --> 00:06:02.009
unsettling glimpse inside the mind of Cletus
Kasady before he even escapes from prison.

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:05.780
After the post-credits scene of the first
"Venom" movie, did Kasady and Brock become

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:06.780
pen pals?

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.610
What exactly does Kasady have to say?

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.509
Will these handwritten exchanges kick off
a kind of cat-and-mouse game between Eddie

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:16.560
and Kasady, where the latter torments the
former from afar until they finally meet in

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:17.560
person again?

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.780
We'll have to wait to know all the details.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.730
At one point in the "Venom" sequel's first
trailer, right after an offscreen Kasady says

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:29.360
how the people who are left behind are "waiting
in the darkness for the rescuer who never

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.030
comes," we see him smash the spider that crawls
across his postcard, killing it instantly.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:38.280
This spider-crushing moment is a clear reference
to the fact that both Carnage and Venom are

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.500
villains of New York's own friendly neighborhood
Spider-Man.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.600
Though Venom aims to fight evil, he's still
a monster.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.099
But Kasady, however, is much more menacing
when infected with a symbiote, becoming Carnage,

00:06:49.099 --> 00:06:51.060
a monster with no moral compass.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:55.000
The main difference between Carnage and Venom
as Spider-Man villains is that Venom, while

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.560
often an antagonist to Spider-Man, has also
teamed up with the heroic web-slinger.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.539
Carnage is an absolute villain who's stronger
than both Venom and Spider-Man, and because

00:07:03.539 --> 00:07:08.090
he's taken over the serial killer Kasady,
he has no love for Spider-Man, hence the smashing.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:13.639
At a couple of points in the "Venom: Let There
Be Carnage" trailer, viewers may see a familiar

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:18.439
face: British actress Naomie Harris, perhaps
most famously associated with the role of

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:22.030
Eve Moneypenny in the James Bond movies "Skyfall"
and "Spectre."

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:26.410
In the "Venom" sequel, Harris is taking the
role of Shriek, who's historically Cletus

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.439
Kasady's love interest.

00:07:27.439 --> 00:07:30.909
Toward the middle of the trailer, we see Harris'
character lying down on her right side, with

00:07:30.909 --> 00:07:34.889
a glimpse of her face revealing what appear
to be scars over her left eye.

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:39.480
These scars seem to match up with the facial
marking that Shriek's human alter-ego Frances

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.360
Barrison is known to have in the pages of
Marvel Comics.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.669
In Marvel canon, Barrison is Kasady's fellow
patient at the Ravencroft Institute for the

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:50.379
Criminally Insane, and he frees her when he
escapes the mental institute.

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:54.659
In the trailer, two figures can be seen walking
away from a blazing structure, suggesting

00:07:54.659 --> 00:07:59.009
that Kasady does destroy Ravencroft in the
"Venom" sequel, and that somebody leaves with

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:00.189
him when he does.

00:08:00.189 --> 00:08:04.319
Later on in the trailer, we see Harris' character
in a holding cell, kneeling on the floor with

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:09.349
her head tilted back and letting out a high-pitched
cry that could most aptly be described as

00:08:09.349 --> 00:08:10.349
a shriek.

00:08:10.349 --> 00:08:13.840
We don't know for certain at this point whether
Kasady and Barrison will be a couple, but

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.729
Andy Serkis did note that Barrison is just
as dangerous as her counterpart.

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:18.749
The director told IGN,

00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:23.199
"She's a damaged soul, and she really has
suffered in her childhood, but there is a

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:26.190
real vulnerability about her, and she's in
a lot of pain.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.669
She's been living in isolation for years — years
and years."

00:08:29.669 --> 00:08:33.200
The filmmaker also referred to the character's
twisted moral code, saying,

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.000
"She has her own sense of fairness and when
that line is crossed, then you see a very,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.640
very dangerous, dark side to her, and that's
what we wanted to do with the character."

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.590
Whether you're addicted to smoking cigarettes
or eating people alive, it can be hard to

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.830
kick old habits.

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:51.770
And it definitely helps to have a safety net
— something to take the edge off those sharp

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.130
turns on the road to becoming a better you.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.750
The trailer for "Venom: Let There Be Carnage"
calls back to one of Venom's more peculiar

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:58.880
cessation aids.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.310
In the '90s Marvel Comics miniseries "Venom:
The Hunger," readers learn that the Venom

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:08.620
symbiote has a doozy of a monkey on its back:
He's addicted to consuming human brains.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:09.970
Why brains?

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:10.970
"Hungry."

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.230
"Whoa, god damn."

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:17.960
Among other things, human brains produce a
chemical called phenethylamine, which Venom

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:22.100
had been milking from Eddie Brock's brain
for years, leaving it dried up.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.950
A few issues of extreme shenanigans later,
and Eddie and Venom symbiote realize that

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:31.750
they could get the same sweet phenethylamine
rush from a bar of delicious chocolate.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.690
The trailer for "Venom: Let There Be Carnage"
shouts out to this storyline in a couple of

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:36.690
ways.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.220
First, Eddie posts around his apartment helpful
reminders to not eat anybody.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:44.570
Then, in the final moments of the trailer,
Mrs. Chen, the protection racket she was running

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:49.100
in the first film now firmly on the other
foot, says she doesn't have the Wonka-licious

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.270
brain substitute that Venom so desperately
needs -- so Venom just brings up the idea

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.270
of eating her instead.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:55.820
"The chocolate delivery hasn’t arrived yet"

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:56.820
NO!"

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:57.820
"We had a deal"

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.910
"You gonna stop protecting me?"

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:01.750
"I am happy to EAT Mrs. Chen"

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.510
"No, no, you can not EAT Mrs. Chen."

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:08.250
It's a silly note to end the trailer on, but
then again, isn't that part of what makes

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:09.890
the "Venom" franchise so much fun?

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:13.910
"Venom: Let There Be Carnage" is set to open
in theaters on September 24.

